---
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="HTML: The Other Language of AI - AI Training Guide">
  <PageShell chapterId="fundamentals" subChapterId="html">
    <div class="prose space-y-6">
      <p>
        The last chapter was about what you send <em>in</em>. This one is about
        what comes back <em>out</em>. Markdown is still the right format for
        prompts, instructions, and config files. But for the things AI{" "}
        <em>produces</em> for you (plans, specs, code reviews, research, reports)
        a clear trend has formed: the output is increasingly HTML, not Markdown.
        Claude Cowork and Claude Code both lean toward generating self-contained
        HTML files you open in a browser.
      </p>

      <p>
        This shift kicked off in public in May 2026, when Thariq Shihipar, an
        engineer on the Claude Code team at Anthropic, posted a short note that
        spread quickly:
      </p>

      <div class="my-8 max-w-xl border border-stone-200 rounded-2xl bg-white px-5 py-4 shadow-sm not-prose">
        <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
          <div
            class="w-11 h-11 rounded-full bg-stone-800 text-white flex items-center justify-center text-base font-semibold shrink-0"
            style="font-family: 'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;"
          >
            T
          </div>
          <div class="min-w-0 flex-1">
            <div class="flex items-start justify-between gap-2">
              <div class="leading-tight">
                <div class="flex items-center gap-1">
                  <span class="text-[15px] font-semibold text-stone-900">Thariq Shihipar</span>
                  <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-label="Verified account" class="w-[18px] h-[18px] text-sky-500 fill-current">
                    <path
                      d="M22.25 12c0-1.43-.88-2.67-2.19-3.34.46-1.39.2-2.9-.81-3.91s-2.52-1.27-3.91-.81c-.66-1.31-1.91-2.19-3.34-2.19s-2.67.88-3.33 2.19c-1.4-.46-2.91-.2-3.92.81s-1.26 2.52-.8 3.91c-1.31.67-2.2 1.91-2.2 3.34s.89 2.67 2.2 3.34c-.46 1.39-.21 2.9.8 3.91s2.52 1.26 3.91.81c.67 1.31 1.91 2.19 3.34 2.19s2.68-.88 3.34-2.19c1.39.45 2.9.2 3.91-.81s1.27-2.52.81-3.91c1.31-.67 2.19-1.91 2.19-3.34zm-11.71 4.2L6.8 12.46l1.41-1.42 2.26 2.26 4.8-5.23 1.47 1.36-6.2 6.77z"
                    ></path>
                  </svg>
                </div>
                <span class="text-sm text-stone-500">@trq212</span>
              </div>
              <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-label="X" class="w-5 h-5 text-stone-900 fill-current shrink-0">
                <path
                  d="M18.244 2.25h3.308l-7.227 8.26 8.502 11.24H16.17l-5.214-6.817L4.99 21.75H1.68l7.73-8.835L1.254 2.25H8.08l4.713 6.231zm-1.161 17.52h1.833L7.084 4.126H5.117z"
                ></path>
              </svg>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-2 text-[15px] text-stone-900 leading-normal m-0">
              HTML is the new markdown. I've stopped writing markdown files for
              almost everything and switched to using Claude Code to generate
              HTML for me. This is why.
            </p>
            <div class="mt-3 pt-3 border-t border-stone-100">
              <a
                href="https://x.com/trq212/status/2052811606032269638"
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
                class="text-sm text-stone-500 no-underline hover:underline"
              >
                May 8, 2026 · Claude Code team, Anthropic
              </a>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        His companion write-up,{" "}
        <a href="https://claude.com/blog/using-claude-code-the-unreasonable-effectiveness-of-html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-orange-600 underline hover:text-orange-700">The Unreasonable Effectiveness of HTML</a>,
        shipped 20 examples of the same content rendered as HTML instead of
        Markdown. The argument is not that Markdown is bad, but that once the AI
        is producing long, detailed output, a plain Markdown report is something
        you read once and abandon around line 15. The same content as a single
        HTML file becomes something you actually use.
      </p>

      <h3>Why HTML for Output</h3>

      <div class="space-y-4 my-8">
        <div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
          <div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-1">
            It holds more kinds of information
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
            Tables, styled sections, diagrams (SVG), code, images, and layout
            all fit naturally in one file. Markdown approximates some of this
            with ASCII and unicode; HTML just does it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
          <div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-1">
            It's navigable, not just a wall of text
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
            Tabs, headings, links, and collapsible sections turn a 500-line spec
            into something you can move around in. A long plan stops being a
            chore to read.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
          <div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-1">
            It's easy to share
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
            A self-contained HTML file opens in any browser. That makes it far
            more likely a teammate actually reviews the plan or report, rather
            than skipping an attachment.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
          <div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-1">
            It can be interactive
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
            Output can include controls: filters, toggles, sliders, even an
            export button that hands your changes back to the AI as the next
            prompt. The document becomes a small interface, not a dead file.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        Underneath all of these is the point Thariq lands on: HTML output keeps
        you more <em>in the loop</em>. You can see what the AI did, poke at it,
        and steer, instead of scrolling past a report you'll never finish.
      </p>

      <h3>What This Means Day to Day</h3>

      <p>
        You mostly don't have to do anything special. When you ask Cowork or
        Claude Code for a plan, a review, or a summary, expect it to offer (or
        default to) a rendered HTML artifact you open in the browser. If you want
        it, ask for it directly: "give me this as a single self-contained HTML
        file." If you'd rather have plain text to paste into Jira or Confluence,
        ask for Markdown instead. The format is a choice you can make per task.
      </p>

      <Callout variant="tip" title="The trade-off">
        <p>
          HTML output is not free. It typically costs more tokens than the same
          content in Markdown, so it's worth reaching for when the output is
          meant to be read, shared, or explored, and skipping it for quick
          throwaway answers. And to be clear, this is about output. What you
          write to the AI (prompts, AGENTS.md, skills) still lives happily in
          Markdown.
        </p>
      </Callout>

      <h3>Further Reading</h3>

      <div class="space-y-3 my-8">
        <div class="flex gap-3 items-start">
          <div class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-orange-400 shrink-0 mt-2"></div>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
            <a href="https://claude.com/blog/using-claude-code-the-unreasonable-effectiveness-of-html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="font-semibold">The Unreasonable Effectiveness of HTML</a> — Thariq Shihipar's original write-up with the 20 side-by-side examples.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="flex gap-3 items-start">
          <div class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-orange-400 shrink-0 mt-2"></div>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
            <a href="https://www.lennysnewsletter.com/p/html-is-the-new-markdown-how-anthropic" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="font-semibold">HTML is the new Markdown</a> — a longer interview on how Anthropic engineers use this in practice.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </PageShell>
</MainLayout>
